{
  "summary": "The previous proposal correctly identifies missing HTTP status checks in smartPollBoard() and smartPollFeatures() as a likely cause, but has three significant weaknesses. First, the htmx claim is wrong: htmx 2.x (the version loaded, 2.0.4) does NOT swap content on non-2xx responses by default, so the client-grid htmx polling is actually safe — the htmx:responseError handler at line 480 already covers the 401 case, and other errors are simply ignored by htmx. Second, the proposed fix of `if (!r.ok) return;` is too simplistic — it silently swallows errors, leaving the user staring at a frozen/stale dashboard with zero indication that polling has broken. A better fix would combine the status check with a visible staleness indicator (e.g., a subtle banner or dimming the board after N consecutive failures) and an exponential backoff to avoid hammering a struggling server every 5 seconds. Third, the proposal overlooks that a bad response gets cached in `_lastBoardHTML` — if the server returns error HTML that gets rendered, the NEXT successful poll will correctly detect a difference and restore the board, but if `_lastBoardHTML` is set to the error text and the server keeps returning the same error, subsequent polls will see html === _lastBoardHTML and do nothing, locking in the broken state. The fix should also skip updating `_lastBoardHTML` when the response is not a valid board fragment. The service worker concern is a red herring — the default service worker registration without explicit cache strategies does not intercept or cache fetch responses."
}
